Cell["\<\
The above plot shows that the density distribution will decline until it \
asymptotically approaches the steady state density distribution.

To illustrate this better, superimpose our two solutions starting with \
different initial density distributions.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"0daaa116-1090-41c2-b49a-f28e423848c1"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"Show", "[", "\[IndentingNewLine]", "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
  RowBox[{
  "graph1", ",", "\[IndentingNewLine]", "graph2", ",", "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
   "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
   RowBox[{"PlotRange", "->", "All"}]}], "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
  "\[IndentingNewLine]", "]"}]], "Input",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.398497015027*^9, 3.3984970934049997`*^9}},
 CellLabel->
  "In[1554]:=",ExpressionUUID->"67c41e44-5f2e-4562-b006-031b5341b992"],

Cell["\<\
The above non-equilibrium cases for limited population growth illustrate that:

Many steady state solutions are possible (unlike unlimited population growth \
which allowed extremely limited steady state solutions).

Non-equilibrium conditions will asymptotically approach steady state \
solutions.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"8d7a56df-6b40-4ffc-8f52-f856289ddfeb"]
